1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,513
Imagine a place so dry that some weather stations

2
00:00:03,513 --> 00:00:07,099
have gone years without recording a single drop of

3
00:00:07,099 --> 00:00:10,827
rain. Where the soil looks like Mars, and scientists

4
00:00:10,827 --> 00:00:14,341
come to test robots meant for other planets. This

5
00:00:14,341 --> 00:00:17,783
is the Atacama Desert in Chile, the driest place

6
00:00:17,783 --> 00:00:21,153
on Earth outside the poles. And today we'll see

7
00:00:21,153 --> 00:00:23,232
why you'd never survive here.

8
00:00:23,782 --> 00:00:27,250
Welcome to Earth Decoded. If you love the most

9
00:00:27,250 --> 00:00:31,547
extreme places on our planet, tap subscribe. Today, we're

10
00:00:31,547 --> 00:00:34,939
going to the driest place on Earth that isn't

11
00:00:34,939 --> 00:00:36,070
covered in ice.

12
00:00:36,620 --> 00:00:41,025
The Atacama stretches about one thousand kilometers along the

13
00:00:41,025 --> 00:00:44,925
Pacific coast of northern Chile, between the ocean and

14
00:00:44,925 --> 00:00:46,369
the Andes mountains.

15
00:00:46,919 --> 00:00:50,272
Some parts get less than one millimeter of rain

16
00:00:50,272 --> 00:00:53,126
a year on average. The city of Arica, at

17
00:00:53,126 --> 00:00:56,621
its northern edge, is one of the driest inhabited

18
00:00:56,621 --> 00:00:58,119
places on the planet.

19
00:00:58,669 --> 00:01:02,039
Why so dry? It's trapped. On one side, the

20
00:01:02,039 --> 00:01:05,810
Andes block rain clouds from the Amazon. On the

21
00:01:05,810 --> 00:01:09,741
other, a cold ocean current along the coast cools

22
00:01:09,741 --> 00:01:12,870
the air, so it can't rise and form rain

23
00:01:12,870 --> 00:01:13,432
clouds.

24
00:01:13,982 --> 00:01:17,644
That cold water is the Humboldt Current, flowing up

25
00:01:17,644 --> 00:01:21,378
from Antarctica. It makes the coast foggy but almost

26
00:01:21,378 --> 00:01:22,025
rainless.

27
00:01:22,575 --> 00:01:26,022
The result is a landscape with almost no plants

28
00:01:26,022 --> 00:01:29,762
at all. In the driest areas, even bacteria struggle

29
00:01:29,762 --> 00:01:31,449
to survive in the soil.

30
00:01:31,999 --> 00:01:35,476
Here's a strange twist. When rare heavy rain fell

31
00:01:35,476 --> 00:01:39,094
on the driest part of the Atacama, scientists found

32
00:01:39,094 --> 00:01:42,571
it actually killed most of the native microbes in

33
00:01:42,571 --> 00:01:45,835
the soil. They were so adapted to dryness that

34
00:01:45,835 --> 00:01:47,679
too much water was deadly.

35
00:01:48,229 --> 00:01:51,599
Quick question. Could you live somewhere where it never

36
00:01:51,599 --> 00:01:53,499
rains? Tell me in the comments.

37
00:01:54,049 --> 00:01:57,253
So what would kill you first? Thirst. With no

38
00:01:57,253 --> 00:02:00,529
rivers, no rain and almost no plants, there is

39
00:02:00,529 --> 00:02:03,235
simply no water to find in most of the

40
00:02:03,235 --> 00:02:03,734
desert.

41
00:02:04,284 --> 00:02:07,407
Then the sun. Much of the Atacama sits high

42
00:02:07,407 --> 00:02:10,894
up, above two thousand meters, under some of the

43
00:02:10,894 --> 00:02:15,107
clearest skies on Earth. The ultraviolet radiation here is

44
00:02:15,107 --> 00:02:19,175
among the strongest measured anywhere on the planet, and

45
00:02:19,175 --> 00:02:22,225
it can burn unprotected skin very quickly.

46
00:02:22,775 --> 00:02:25,720
Then the cold. Days can be warm, but at

47
00:02:25,720 --> 00:02:28,968
night, with no clouds and dry air, the high

48
00:02:28,968 --> 00:02:31,309
desert can drop below freezing.

49
00:02:31,859 --> 00:02:35,296
And the altitude. On the high plateau, the air

50
00:02:35,296 --> 00:02:39,481
has much less oxygen. Workers at some observatories here

51
00:02:39,481 --> 00:02:43,517
carry oxygen tanks and must pass medical checks before

52
00:02:43,517 --> 00:02:44,189
going up.

53
00:02:44,739 --> 00:02:48,405
And it's empty. Between towns, you can drive for

54
00:02:48,405 --> 00:02:51,841
hours without seeing another car, a tree or a

55
00:02:51,841 --> 00:02:55,200
house. If you broke down off the main roads,

56
00:02:55,200 --> 00:02:57,262
nobody might pass for days.

57
00:02:57,812 --> 00:03:01,002
The Atacama is so much like Mars that NASA

58
00:03:01,002 --> 00:03:05,635
and other space agencies test rovers and life-detection tools

59
00:03:05,635 --> 00:03:08,900
here. If an instrument can find life in the

60
00:03:08,900 --> 00:03:12,166
driest Atacama soil, it may be able to find

61
00:03:12,166 --> 00:03:13,001
it on Mars.

62
00:03:13,551 --> 00:03:16,529
Places like the Valley of the Moon, near San

63
00:03:16,529 --> 00:03:19,845
Pedro de Atacama, look so alien that they've been

64
00:03:19,845 --> 00:03:21,807
used to imagine other worlds.

65
00:03:22,357 --> 00:03:25,300
The desert is also a great place to find

66
00:03:25,300 --> 00:03:29,199
meteorites. Space rocks that fall here barely rust or

67
00:03:29,199 --> 00:03:32,510
erode, and they stand out on the bare ground,

68
00:03:32,510 --> 00:03:35,968
so scientists have collected thousands of them.

69
00:03:36,518 --> 00:03:39,785
High in the Andes, at more than four thousand

70
00:03:39,785 --> 00:03:43,416
meters, the El Tatio geysers puff columns of steam

71
00:03:43,416 --> 00:03:46,974
into the freezing morning air, one of the highest

72
00:03:46,974 --> 00:03:48,934
geyser fields in the world.

73
00:03:49,484 --> 00:03:52,707
The same dry, clear skies make the Atacama the

74
00:03:52,707 --> 00:03:55,581
best place on Earth to look at the stars.

75
00:03:55,581 --> 00:03:59,435
That's why some of the world's most powerful telescopes

76
00:03:59,435 --> 00:04:00,065
are here.

77
00:04:00,615 --> 00:04:04,925
ALMA, a giant array of sixty-six radio antennas, sits

78
00:04:04,925 --> 00:04:09,479
at about five thousand meters on the Chajnantor plateau.

79
00:04:09,479 --> 00:04:13,057
It studies cold gas and dust where stars and

80
00:04:13,057 --> 00:04:14,439
planets are born.

81
00:04:14,989 --> 00:04:18,944
Nearby, on Cerro Paranal, the Very Large Telescope has

82
00:04:18,944 --> 00:04:22,678
helped scientists study the giant black hole at the

83
00:04:22,678 --> 00:04:26,486
center of our galaxy. And on a neighboring mountain,

84
00:04:26,486 --> 00:04:30,294
the Extremely Large Telescope is being built, with a

85
00:04:30,294 --> 00:04:32,931
main mirror thirty-nine meters wide.

86
00:04:33,481 --> 00:04:37,267
Some estimates say that by the 2030s, Chile could

87
00:04:37,267 --> 00:04:41,363
host around forty percent of the world's capacity for

88
00:04:41,363 --> 00:04:46,154
large astronomical observation. The desert has become a window

89
00:04:46,154 --> 00:04:47,390
to the universe.

90
00:04:47,940 --> 00:04:50,888
Would you rather visit the Atacama for the stars

91
00:04:50,888 --> 00:04:53,529
or for the desert? Tell me in the comments.

92
00:04:54,079 --> 00:04:57,247
And yet, life finds a way. Along the coast,

93
00:04:57,247 --> 00:05:01,077
people use fog catchers, big nets that trap droplets

94
00:05:01,077 --> 00:05:05,276
from the morning fog, called camanchaca, and collect them

95
00:05:05,276 --> 00:05:06,602
as drinking water.

96
00:05:07,152 --> 00:05:11,098
Sometimes, rare rains arrive, often linked to El Niño

97
00:05:11,098 --> 00:05:14,448
years. When they do, seeds that waited in the

98
00:05:14,448 --> 00:05:18,469
ground for years burst into bloom, covering the desert

99
00:05:18,469 --> 00:05:22,340
in pink, purple and yellow flowers. Chileans call it

100
00:05:22,340 --> 00:05:25,541
the desierto florido, the flowering desert.

101
00:05:26,091 --> 00:05:29,489
On the salt lakes of the high plateau, flamingos

102
00:05:29,489 --> 00:05:32,462
feed on tiny algae and shrimp in the salty

103
00:05:32,462 --> 00:05:35,435
water, bright pink against the white salt.

104
00:05:35,985 --> 00:05:39,593
And wild vicuñas, relatives of llamas with some of

105
00:05:39,593 --> 00:05:42,623
the finest wool in the world, graze on the

106
00:05:42,623 --> 00:05:44,860
sparse grass of the high Andes.

107
00:05:45,410 --> 00:05:49,285
But rain can also be dangerous. In March 2015,

108
00:05:49,285 --> 00:05:54,171
unusual storms caused deadly floods and mudslides in towns

109
00:05:54,171 --> 00:05:58,719
of the Atacama region, because the dry ground couldn't

110
00:05:58,719 --> 00:06:00,151
absorb the water.

111
00:06:00,701 --> 00:06:04,263
People have lived here for thousands of years. The

112
00:06:04,263 --> 00:06:08,822
Chinchorro culture, along the coast, mummified their dead around

113
00:06:08,822 --> 00:06:12,882
seven thousand years ago, about two thousand years before

114
00:06:12,882 --> 00:06:16,515
the Egyptians. Their mummies are now a UNESCO World

115
00:06:16,515 --> 00:06:17,512
Heritage Site.

116
00:06:18,062 --> 00:06:21,490
The desert is also rich in minerals. Chile is

117
00:06:21,490 --> 00:06:26,060
the world's largest producer of copper, and Chuquicamata, in

118
00:06:26,060 --> 00:06:29,792
the Atacama, is one of the biggest open-pit mines

119
00:06:29,792 --> 00:06:30,478
on Earth.

120
00:06:31,028 --> 00:06:34,499
The Salar de Atacama, a huge salt flat, holds

121
00:06:34,499 --> 00:06:38,509
some of the world's largest reserves of lithium, the

122
00:06:38,509 --> 00:06:42,057
metal inside phone and electric car batteries.

123
00:06:42,607 --> 00:06:46,343
Not everything left in the desert is natural. In

124
00:06:46,343 --> 00:06:51,247
recent years, reports showed huge piles of unwanted second-hand

125
00:06:51,247 --> 00:06:54,827
clothes dumped in the Atacama near the city of

126
00:06:54,827 --> 00:06:58,485
Alto Hospicio, a reminder of how much waste the

127
00:06:58,485 --> 00:06:59,653
world produces.

128
00:07:00,203 --> 00:07:04,085
And over a century ago, the desert's saltpeter, used

129
00:07:04,085 --> 00:07:07,744
for fertilizer and explosives, made it one of the

130
00:07:07,744 --> 00:07:11,925
richest regions in the world, leaving behind ghost towns

131
00:07:11,925 --> 00:07:13,195
like Humberstone.

132
00:07:13,745 --> 00:07:17,339
So, could you survive the Atacama? Alone, with no

133
00:07:17,339 --> 00:07:21,227
water, under that sun, almost certainly not. But with

134
00:07:21,227 --> 00:07:25,849
fog nets, mines, telescopes and ancient traditions, people have

135
00:07:25,849 --> 00:07:28,857
found ways to live in the driest place on

136
00:07:28,857 --> 00:07:29,297
Earth.

137
00:07:29,847 --> 00:07:33,815
Last question. Which extreme place should we explore next?

138
00:07:33,815 --> 00:07:35,457
Tell me in the comments.

139
00:07:36,007 --> 00:07:39,743
Thanks for watching Earth Decoded. If you enjoyed this,

140
00:07:39,743 --> 00:07:42,868
like the video, subscribe, and share it with a

141
00:07:42,868 --> 00:07:45,721
friend who loves the stars. See you in the

142
00:07:45,721 --> 00:07:46,333
next one.

